<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diyar Bakr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Diyar Bakr province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diyar Bakr was the medieval Arabic name for the northernmost province of the Jazira, the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> basin around Amid (modern Diyarbakır) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. Named after the Arab tribe of Bakr ibn Wa'il, it was contested by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> before the Kurdish Marwanid dynasty ruled it from the 980s to the 1080s, when it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>.</p></div>
